263
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:47,620
Inflation.

264
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:49,640
Well, no, these are real rates of return, actually,

265
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:50,740
so it's not inflation either.

266
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:52,840
How is it possible?

267
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:55,620
The answer is finite lifespan finally kicks in.

268
00:10:55,620 --> 00:10:59,380
So if the console stopped paying in 100 years

269
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:02,340
and you paid a billion for it and it paid a dollar a year,

270
00:11:02,620 --> 00:11:04,260
then you've got a negative rate of return.

271
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:05,920
Then it's massive.

272
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:09,860
It's like negative 99.999999% negative rate of return

273
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:15,400
because you died before you actually could finally get

274
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:16,380
your money back.

275
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:21,420
So if you pay a billion and it pays a dollar a year,

276
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:22,720
just live for a billion years

277
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:24,360
and a billion years you've recouped your money.

278
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:28,060
And the same would be true for education.

279
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:30,620
As long as there's any actual effect of productivity

280
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:33,200
and you live forever, it would have

281
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:34,440
to have a positive real rate of return.

282
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:36,300
However, we don't live forever.

283
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:40,880
So and again, why does it matter here?

284
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:44,000
Well, because once you start getting noticeable negative

285
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,580
rates of return, then finally the fact that you only live

286
00:11:46,580 --> 00:11:51,040
for 40 years really kicks in in the calculations, right?

287
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:52,680
Because once you have a substantially negative rate

288
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:54,620
of return, then it might be that you have to be making money,

289
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:56,560
that you have to benefit from it for 1,000 years.

290
00:11:56,560 --> 00:11:58,740
And if you need to benefit for 1,000 years

291
00:11:58,740 --> 00:12:01,460
and you only do it for 40, you're really out of luck.

292
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:03,540
All right.

293
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:08,000
Now, there is a simple policy governments could use

294
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,140
to improve market efficiency.

295
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:10,660
What do we normally recommend

296
00:12:11,020 --> 00:12:12,220
when there's a negative externality?

297
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:13,760
That is not Mason economists,

298
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:15,020
but a normal economist recommend

299
00:12:15,020 --> 00:12:16,100
when there's a negative externality.

300
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:17,440
Tax it. Yes.

301
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:19,640
So you could go and tax education.

302
00:12:19,640 --> 00:12:21,820
Right? And again, the signaling model,

303
00:12:21,820 --> 00:12:23,680
they're signaling waste real resources.

304
00:12:24,420 --> 00:12:26,140
And what does taxing education do?

305
00:12:26,380 --> 00:12:28,560
It preserves the relative ranking of everybody.

306
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:31,400
So the most educated before the tax is still going

307
00:12:31,400 --> 00:12:33,380
to be the most educated after the tax and so on.

308
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:36,640
But it reduces the waste of the real resources.

309
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:37,780
It means that you could go

310
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:41,100
and start your life one degree earlier.

311
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:44,920
You could go and be a professor with a bachelor's degree.

312
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:48,240
You could go and be a manager with a high school degree.

313
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:50,200
You could go and get a government job

314
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:51,700
as a high school dropout.

315
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:55,640
Right? This is not how the real world works anymore.

316
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:57,620
Yes. So in 1960, about a third

317
00:12:57,620 --> 00:12:59,560
of government employees were high school dropouts.

318
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:01,420
Now it's down to like 3%.

319
00:13:01,420 --> 00:13:03,180
Did I mention this last week

320
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:04,480
when I was doing conventional inflation?

321
00:13:05,180 --> 00:13:08,000
So anyway, this is the kind of thing that happens

322
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,720
when the education you receive really has nothing,

323
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:11,620
really has very little to do

324
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:13,120
with actually learning how to do the job.

325
00:13:13,580 --> 00:13:16,660
And yet, the employer wants to go and have a standard

326
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:18,740
of quality and say, all right, fine.

327
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:20,240
You have to have education.

328
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:22,620
You can't be in the bottom third of educational attainment.

329
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:25,840
So now almost no government employees are high school

330
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:26,320
dropouts.

331
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:27,780
And the ones that are left are probably really old.

332
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:30,120
They're the people grandfathered in.

333
00:13:30,900 --> 00:13:32,320
Because, you know, you wouldn't want your garbage collected

334
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:33,760
by someone who didn't finish high school, would you?

335
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:35,360
You know, that would be terrible.

336
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:37,180
Right? Right?

337
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:38,420
Lord only knows what would occur.

338
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:42,420
Right. So. All right.

339
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:44,620
Now, in the real world, how often do governments

340
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:47,580
tax education?

341
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:48,820
Never. Almost never.

342
00:13:49,220 --> 00:13:51,340
You know, they'll be taxed on certain kinds of education

343
00:13:51,340 --> 00:13:53,500
or prohibitions on certain kinds of education sometimes.

344
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:56,720
But still, this really is extremely unusual

345
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:57,440
to tax education.

346
00:13:57,440 --> 00:13:59,820
Instead, real world governments almost do, always do what

347
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:00,520
with education?

348
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:01,140
Subsidize it.

349
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:01,840
Subsidize it.

350
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:03,700
So it's like subsidizing pollution.

351
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,240
It's like subsidizing not vaccinating your kids.

352
00:14:07,580 --> 00:14:08,900
Something like that.

353
00:14:09,460 --> 00:14:11,820
Right? Where you're giving people incentives to go

354
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:13,880
and do something that is socially harmful

355
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:15,080
that they were going to do anyway.

356
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:19,420
But now, let's encourage them to do even more of it.

357
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:22,660
Okay. Now, whenever people start thinking about things

358
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:24,620
like taxing education or even just getting rid

359
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:27,060
of the subsidies, there's a distinctive

360
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:28,480
and very natural reaction of horror.

361
00:14:28,480 --> 00:14:31,460
Because in the modern economy, it is pretty close to true.

362
00:14:31,460 --> 00:14:33,220
You need a college degree to get a good job.

363
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:35,960
And there's plenty of people that couldn't afford to go

364
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:38,620
and pay an unsubsidized tuition for four years and give

365
00:14:38,620 --> 00:14:39,600
up the earnings for four years.

366
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:42,460
It'd be very big financial burden on a lot

367
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:44,560
of people who currently do it.

368
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:48,300
The key signaling insight is to say, yes, it's true.

369
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:50,220
If education were unsubsidized,

370
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:51,340
a lot of us couldn't afford it.

371
00:14:52,180 --> 00:14:52,880
That's the bad news.

372
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:55,540
But there's very important offsetting good news.

373
00:14:56,380 --> 00:14:57,260
You couldn't afford it.

374
00:14:57,260 --> 00:14:58,820
You also wouldn't need it anymore.

375
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:02,740
You could go and get the job that you want

376
00:15:02,740 --> 00:15:03,960
with one fewer degree.

377
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:05,600
Is that possible?

378
00:15:05,880 --> 00:15:06,720
Not only is it possible,

379
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:08,500
it's what happened two generations ago.

380
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:13,260
Two generations ago, the whole educational distribution was

381
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:14,920
shifted downward about a full degree.

382
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:19,480
So back in those days, people could get hired

383
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:21,300
to be professors at top schools right

384
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:22,980
out of undergraduate sometimes.

385
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:24,460
Master's degree maybe.

386
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:27,900
I'd say, like, to advance, you might need to get the PhD.

387
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:31,180
But still, you know, like, you didn't really need it.

388
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:33,300
So, like, even, like, a professor didn't really need the

389
00:15:33,300 --> 00:15:35,480
degree that much in order to go and be a professor.

390
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:38,360
Right? And then, you know, like, it was very standard

391
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:40,380
for someone who was a very successful manager,

392
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:43,220
someone who was a job that you think of as requiring a lot

393
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:45,220
of training and responsibility

394
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:46,500
to just have a high school degree.

395
00:15:47,240 --> 00:15:51,500
Which was a big deal back in 1945.

396
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:54,140
High school degrees still actually put you

397
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:57,960
in the top 25%, right?

398
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:00,000
So for the, like, for all workers.

399
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,400
So that's already starting to get, be the place

400
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:04,460
where young people are more commonly finishing.

401
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:06,840
Right? And, of course, you go back another generation before

402
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:09,320
then, back when there's plenty of people who didn't even finish

403
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:11,020
in high school who were the most successful people

404
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:11,680
in the United States.

405
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:14,900
Right? Where do they learn how to do their jobs

406
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:16,300
if they didn't learn how to do it in high school?

407
00:16:16,840 --> 00:16:18,740
Where do they, yes, on the job.

408
00:16:19,300 --> 00:16:20,780
Where does almost everybody today learn how

409
00:16:20,780 --> 00:16:21,800
to do their jobs, too?

410
00:16:21,860 --> 00:16:22,880
On the job.

411
00:16:23,860 --> 00:16:25,060
Right? So general, you know,

412
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:26,620
general fact about the labor force.

413
00:16:27,460 --> 00:16:30,280
Most people, like, so, you know, so if you're going

414
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:31,700
to be a professor, then you're going to use a lot

415
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:33,460
of what you learned in school on the job.

416
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:36,820
But for everybody else, people go and study a bunch

417
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:38,660
of stuff that professors care about

418
00:16:38,660 --> 00:16:41,360
and is not actually very useful in the outside world.

419
00:16:42,060 --> 00:16:44,780
And then, if you do well enough,

420
00:16:45,020 --> 00:16:47,760
then someone in the real world will give you the opportunity

421
00:16:47,760 --> 00:16:49,920
to get your entry-level job in the real world.

422
00:16:50,560 --> 00:16:53,000
And then, for the first time, you get real job training.

423
00:16:54,780 --> 00:16:58,200
Is a cynical but not too far from the truth story

424
00:16:58,200 --> 00:16:59,420
about what's going on.

425
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:03,220
Right? You know, there's these, you know, common feeling,

426
00:17:03,220 --> 00:17:05,360
like you finish college, like, well, what do I know how to do?

427
00:17:05,360 --> 00:17:09,980
Well, not that much more in the real world

428
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:11,520
that I could do four years ago.

429
00:17:11,820 --> 00:17:13,620
But now, people will give me an interview

430
00:17:13,620 --> 00:17:15,160
who would never have talked to me before.

431
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:18,240
And after I get the interview, they might hire me.

432
00:17:18,240 --> 00:17:20,200
And then, what do I do when I show up?

433
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:22,160
I learn how to do what I've been hired to do

434
00:17:22,160 --> 00:17:23,500
because I didn't learn it before.

435
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:26,080
All right.

436
00:17:27,540 --> 00:17:27,740
Yes?

437
00:17:27,740 --> 00:17:34,560
So, did people stick to one job before than now?

438
00:17:34,560 --> 00:17:40,840
Because they were more trained in the firms and on the job?

439
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:44,120
So, you know, there's some research suggesting

440
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:47,920
that there's more job switches now than there were in the past.

441
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:50,780
Although, I think that research is also in question

442
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:51,920
in a lot of ways.

443
00:17:51,920 --> 00:17:54,360
So, it's not clear that people actually did change more

444
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:57,780
than in the past than they do right now.

445
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:00,680
You know, it looks like, you know, it's fairly common

446
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:01,840
to change jobs right now.

447
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:04,100
What's less common is changing your whole occupation.

448
00:18:05,940 --> 00:18:07,900
Right now, sometimes people will start off

449
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:09,760
as like a practicing engineer,

450
00:18:09,760 --> 00:18:11,700
and then they become a manager of engineering.

451
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:14,640
So, that's one where, in a way, you're changing your occupation

452
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:17,420
where you're moving along a pretty clear career path.

453
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:19,480
So, it's not so surprising.

454
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:23,180
There is this research on whether

455
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:25,900
or not there's a return to just staying at the same firm.

456
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:29,700
And the least, well, the most interesting thing

457
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:30,520
that I read is that they say, you know,

458
00:18:30,520 --> 00:18:32,820
the real return is in staying in the same occupation.

459
00:18:34,340 --> 00:18:37,860
So, if you move from job to job fairly frequently,

460
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:39,740
but you stay in the same industry,

461
00:18:39,740 --> 00:18:41,300
then basically you're acquiring more skills.

462
00:18:41,300 --> 00:18:42,700
Each job you're acquiring more skills

463
00:18:42,700 --> 00:18:43,720
that are relevant to the next job.

464
00:18:44,160 --> 00:18:45,740
But if you totally switch your field,

465
00:18:46,140 --> 00:18:48,740
that's where you have lost a lot of what you've learned.

466
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:51,720
All right.

467
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:53,100
So, what would happen in a world

468
00:18:53,100 --> 00:18:55,020
where there was much less formal education?

469
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:58,380
Well, a lot of firms would switch to apprenticeships,

470
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:00,640
right, and other ways of finding out your type.

471
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:03,460
So, this is, of course,

472
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:05,380
used in many other countries more frequently.

473
00:19:05,380 --> 00:19:07,260
So, Germany, Switzerland make a lot more,

474
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:08,900
rely a lot more on apprenticeships.

475
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:11,880
So, that's one obvious thing.

476
00:19:11,880 --> 00:19:13,860
And again, why would they do it in a world

477
00:19:13,860 --> 00:19:16,300
where formal educational credentials are so much lower?

478
00:19:16,300 --> 00:19:19,100
Well, if you can't sort people out by their credentials,

479
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:20,460
you want to figure out some way of doing it.

480
00:19:21,220 --> 00:19:23,900
So, obvious thing is to do something like an apprenticeship.

481
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:26,500
Or it might be that you would actually go

482
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:29,540
and have people pay you to get an apprenticeship.

483
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:32,180
Right now, we have something kind of like that

484
00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:33,420
where we have the unpaid apprenticeship.

485
00:19:34,540 --> 00:19:35,540
But you could actually go further

486
00:19:35,540 --> 00:19:37,340
and have the negatively paid apprenticeship.

487
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:41,180
Many people regard this as so offensive and horrifying.

488
00:19:42,420 --> 00:19:45,580
Well, so, it's perfectly fine to go and pay tuition

489
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:49,300
for four years. That's fine. Right?

490
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:51,780
But it's not fine to go and pay that tuition to a place

491
00:19:51,780 --> 00:19:53,100
that actually would teach you some job skills

492
00:19:53,100 --> 00:19:54,140
during that time.

493
00:19:54,140 --> 00:19:57,460
That would be an offense against God if that occurred.

494
00:19:57,460 --> 00:19:59,020
Yes?

495
00:19:59,020 --> 00:20:19,020
Ah, thank you very much.


